                "review": {
                  "summary": "Intent-phase decision on .fbk/cancellation/prd.md — can this behavior and scope guide the next design/specification phase? Yes. The PRD states a concrete human problem (cancel a job exactly once, releasing its held resource once) and a fully derivable observable contract: first cancel returns True and invokes release once; later cancels return False without re-invoking; cancelled starts False and becomes True before release runs; release exceptions propagate while cancellation stays recorded; independent jobs keep independent state. Scope boundaries are explicit (no async, threads, scheduling, retry, logging, third-party deps), it declares no open product decisions and no separate design doc, and it sets a README documentation obligation. The subtlest interaction is internally consistent rather than contradictory: because cancelled is set before release is called, a raising release leaves the job cancelled and a second cancel correctly returns False without retrying. Feasible as a small synchronous Python class validated by standard-library unittest. The repository contains no other files, so there are no existing conventions to reconcile against; that absence alone does not make context incomplete. No blockers. Two suggestions: the PRD names acceptance observation channels but supplies no worked acceptance examples, and it does not state the argument signature used when invoking release.",
                  "findings": [
                    {
                      "title": "No concrete acceptance examples, only named observation channels",
                      "location": ".fbk/cancellation/prd.md:3",
                      "evidence": "The PRD says \"Acceptance is observable through callback invocations, return values, state, and exception propagation, with standard-library unittest tests\" but gives no worked sequence. Every behavioral rule is stated in prose in line 2, so examples are derivable, but the specification phase must invent the literal sequences itself, which risks drift from the author's intent (e.g. whether the repeat-cancel case is checked for both return value and unchanged call count).",
                      "remedy": "Add two or three literal acceptance examples to the PRD, e.g. fresh job: cancelled is False, calls == 0; cancel() -> True, calls == 1, cancelled True; cancel() again -> False, calls still 1; and a raising-release case: first cancel propagates the exception with cancelled True and calls == 1, second cancel -> False with calls still 1.",
                      "severity": "suggestion"
                    },
                    {
                      "title": "Invocation signature of the release callable is unstated",
                      "location": ".fbk/cancellation/prd.md:2",
                      "evidence": "The PRD says the Job is \"initialized with a callable release function\" and that cancel \"calls release once\", but never states that release is invoked with no arguments. A spec author could reasonably pass the job itself or a reason argument, which would change the public contract and every caller's callback shape.",
                      "severity": "suggestion",
                      "remedy": "State in the PRD (or pin in the specification phase) that release is invoked with no arguments and that its return value is ignored."
                    }
                  ]
                },
